      "content": "### Chapter 37: Hoi An: The Timeless Essence\n\n![Hoi An - Iconic cityscape](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-01/images/hoi-an-character.jpg)\n\nHoi An was once Southeast Asia's premier maritime trading port, where merchant fleets from China, Japan, Portugal, and the Netherlands gathered between the fifteenth and nineteenth centuries. Spared from the devastation of modern warfare, the ancient town remains a remarkably preserved architectural jewel of narrow streets lined with ochre-yellow timber houses and Chinese clan assembly halls.\n\n![Geographic location of Hoi An in Vietnam](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-01/images/hoi-an-map.png)\n\nSpanning a small canal is the iconic Japanese Covered Bridge, built in the 1590s with weathered wooden shingles, curved tiled roofs, and guardian monkey and dog statues. When evening falls, the town bans all motor vehicles and transforms into an enchanting fairy tale: thousands of traditional silk lanterns in rich crimson, gold, and jade glow softly across streets and wooden shopfronts, while candlelit paper lanterns drift down the calm waters of the Thu Bon River."
